{
  "id": "critical-points-gradient-field",
  "title": "Critical Points: Where the Gradient Vanishes",
  "eyebrow": "interior critical points",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 50,
  "topic": "Finding interior critical points by setting the gradient equal to zero. For f(x, y) = x³/3 − x + y² − 2y, plot the contour map plus the gradient vector field; sweep a scanner along the line y = 1 and watch |∇f| dip to zero at exactly two locations — x = −1 and x = +1. Solving the system ∇f = (x² − 1, 2y − 2) = (0, 0) recovers the same pair, with one a local minimum and one a saddle. Classification waits for the Hesse test.",
  "subject_id": "mat2b",
  "chapter_number": 6,
  "prerequisites": [
    "gradient-and-level-curves",
    "hessian-test"
  ],
